      }
    },
    "r2": {
      "O": {
        "1": {
          "evidence": "The title includes the word 'Decreasing.' The plotted line starts near the 1,000 mark at 1880 and ends near the 3,000 mark at 1891. The 1891 endpoint is visibly higher on the page than the 1880 endpoint.",
          "status": "supported"
        }
      },
      "B": {
        "reading_checked": "If conflicting chart signals or the absence of a supplied routing threshold prevents a reliable growth-versus-decline classification, then the conservative available route is routine monitoring. (Assumes routine monitoring is the designated fallback when no explicit routing policy resolves conflicting evidence.)",
        "evidence": "The necessary conditions are: (1) conflicting signals or absence of a threshold prevents reliable classification, and (2) routine monitoring is the designated fallback. The chart presents a clear conflict: the title says 'Decreasing' while the data shows a large net increase from 1880 to 1891. There is no explicit routing policy or threshold provided in the task or on the chart to resolve this. The option 'Keep on routine tourism monitoring' is available. While the assumption that it is the 'designated fallback' is not explicitly legislated, it is the only option that does not commit to a specific trend direction (growth or decline) in the face of contradictory evidence. Thus, the conditions are effectively met by the available options and the lack of a resolving rule.",
        "status": "supported"
      }
    },
    "r3": {
      "O": {
        "1": {
          "evidence": "The blue line starts at the 1880 tick near the 1,000 y-axis tick. It ends at the 1891 tick, which is visibly higher on the page than the 1880 endpoint (near 3,000).",
          "status": "supported"
        },
        "2": {
          "evidence": "The horizontal axis is labeled 'Year' with ticks 1880-1891. The vertical axis is labeled 'Number of Tourists' with ticks 0-3,000.",
          "status": "supported"
        }
      },
      "B": {
        "reading_checked": "If the horizontal axis labeled 'Year' orders the plotted line from 1880 to 1891 and the vertical axis labeled 'Number of Tourists' uses the visible numeric ticks with larger values higher on the page, then a line endpoint higher at 1891 than at 1880 indicates a higher new-tourist count at the end than at the start. If routing is based on overall endpoint growth, that interpretation supports growth planning. (Assumes the single blue line represents Town Z new tourists for the labeled years and that endpoint change, rather than the title or only the latest year-to-year movement, is the routing criterion.)",
        "evidence": "The necessary conditions are: (1) standard axis interpretation (Year on X, Number on Y, higher is larger), (2) the line represents the data, and (3) routing is based on 'overall endpoint growth'. Conditions 1 and 2 are supported by the chart's labels and standard conventions. Condition 3 is the critical assumption. The task asks to 'choose the follow-up route for the tourism operations trend'. It does not specify that 'endpoint growth' is the criterion. Furthermore, the title explicitly states 'Decreasing', which directly contradicts the 'growth' interpretation derived from endpoints. Because the routing criterion is not defined as 'endpoint growth' and is contradicted by the title, the condition that this specific interpretation drives the routing is not supported.",
        "status": "unclear"
      }
    }
